        "title":  "Don\u0027t be fooled--LLMs don\u0027t reason",
        "source":  "MIT Technology Review",
        "url":  "https://www.technologyreview.com/2026/10/02/1145639/dont-be-fooled-llms-dont-reason/",
        "date":  "2026-10-02",
        "theme":  "Philosophy",
        "summary":  "Google DeepMind researcher Thore Graepel argues that large language models do not reason--they generate plausible next tokens without maintaining an explicit epistemic state, which means they cannot separate knowledge from manipulation and frequently fabricate post-hoc reasoning chains. He contrasts LLMs with AlphaGo\u0027s dual architecture, which combined intuitive pattern recognition with explicit game-tree search, and proposes a similar hybrid approach for future AI systems that can maintain auditable reasoning records and systematically revise beliefs. The article positions this gap as a fundamental barrier to trustworthy AI in high-stakes domains like medicine and scientific research.",
        "execSummary":  "A senior AI researcher at Google DeepMind makes a technically grounded case that current LLMs produce outputs that look like reasoning but are structurally incapable of it--a distinction that has direct implications for how enterprises should scope AI decision-making authority. The practical risk is that AI systems deployed in high-stakes workflows (claims adjudication, escalation routing, compliance review) may produce confident-sounding but structurally unreliable justifications that humans then accept. For enterprise leaders, this reinforces the need to treat LLM outputs as probabilistic drafts requiring human verification, not as auditable reasoning chains--especially in regulated or high-accountability contexts. The key watch point is whether next-generation architectures that combine LLMs with explicit reasoning engines (the AlphaGo hybrid model) gain traction, which would change the calculus significantly.",
